From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tom Englund <tomenglund26@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:43:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms: add low framerate compensation for VRR

Below the display's minimum refresh rate, the hardware repeats the current
frame when SET_DISPLAY_RATE's MIN_REFRESH_INTERVAL expires, however close the
next frame is. A flip that arrives during that scanout has to wait for it to
finish, up to a whole scanout.

When the frame rate gets close to the minimum, predict the next frame's duration
and repeat the current frame at even intervals through the cursor channel's
RELEASE_ELV, like cursor motion does. That leaves the scanout idle when the next
frame is due. The hardware timer stays armed, so a frame that takes longer than
predicted is no worse off than without LFC.

The thresholds and the choice of multiplier follow amdgpu's
apply_below_the_range(). Unlike amdgpu's repeats, these are timed by software, and
a wrong one costs a real frame up to a whole scanout, so:

- Predict with the shorter of the last two frames, so that a single slow frame
  or uneven frame pacing falls back to the hardware timer.
- Time repeats from the flip, not from the last repeat, so that timer latency
  doesn't add up.
- Skip a repeat if its timer ran so late that it could collide with the next
  frame, or if the last flip is still pending, e.g. on an acquire fence.
- Stop for 2 s after 5 new frames in a row arrive during a repeat's scanout.

Displays with a maximum refresh rate below twice the minimum, displays without a
minimum refresh rate and tearing flips don't use LFC. Disable with
/sys/module/nvidia_modeset/parameters/vrr_lfc. nvkms_vrr_lfc_event() is a kprobe
point for what LFC does, because functions in the NVKMS core can't be probed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
